You don't have to change this, but there is a newer way to handle the situation where a data member initially has no value.

You can use a std::optional<uint64_t> to represent two possible states:
* Undefined value (I don't know the position value)
* Defined value (e.g. 1-8 or whatever)

e.g.
```
  std::optional<uint64_t> chassisPathPositionId;

  ...

  if (chassisPathPositionId)
  {
    // ... some code that only should happen if the position is defined/known
  }
```

Since this method is in a chassis class, we probably don't need Chassis in the method name?

Usually this method is just called 'isPresent'.  e.g.
```
  if (chassis.isPresent())
  {
    // Do stuff that only applies to present chassis
  }
```

As mentioned in a previous file, boolean variables normally start with is/was/has, so this should probably be isMultiChassisSystem.

Then if statements read a little nicer:
```
  if (isMultiChassisSystem)
  {
    // Do stuff that only applies for multi-chassis systems
```
